PS(1)                          P32W Unix                           PS(1)

NAME
       ps - report process status

SYNOPSIS
       ps [-u user] [-t tty] [-f]

DESCRIPTION
       ps prints one line for each process in the system (all
       users', all terminals') under a heading:

       PID    the process id
       PPID   the parent's process id (0 if none)
       S      the state: R running or ready to run, S sleeping
              (waiting for an event), Z zombie (exited, not yet
              waited for), T stopped (traced by db(1)), E being
              created
       UID    the real user id; with -f, USER, the login name
       TTY    the controlling terminal, 0 for /dev/tty0 and 1 for
              /dev/tty1; ? if it has none
       SIZE   the size of its memory, program, data and stack, in
              bytes
       TIME   the CPU time used, user and system, as minutes:
              seconds
       CMD    the last component of the file it is running, up to
              15 characters

       The information is read from the kernel's process table one
       slot at a time, so it is not an exact snapshot: a process can
       start or end while ps runs.

OPTIONS
       -u user
              only the processes of user, given as a login name or a
              number
       -t tty only the processes whose controlling terminal is tty
              (0 or 1, or tty0 or tty1)
       -f     show the user's login name instead of the uid

EXAMPLES
       ps                            everything
       ps -f -u ken                  ken's processes, by name
       ps -t 1                       what runs on the second terminal

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 if the user named with -u is neither a login name nor
       a number ("ps: no user x"); 2 for a usage error or a -t
       that is not a number.

SEE ALSO
       kill(1), top(1), who(1), uptime(1), passwd(5)

BUGS
       There are no BSD-style or System V options (aux, -e, -l);
       the command's arguments are not shown, only its name.

       Options cannot be combined (-fu is an error), and only the
       last -u or -t counts.
